definition_long:
The defender's target, as the Lucena is the attacker's. Its conditions, from the defender's side:

  - the defending king is on the pawn's queening square or adjacent to it;
  - the pawn has not yet reached the defender's third rank;
  - the attacking king is beyond the defender's third rank;
  - the defending rook sits on the third rank, denying that rank to the attacking king.

The method has TWO PHASES and the timing is the whole point. Hold the rook on the third rank, which stops the attacking king coming to the sixth. When the pawn is finally pushed to the third rank, the rook drops to the far end of the board — seventh or eighth — and checks from behind, where the attacking king has no shelter because its own pawn no longer screens it.

Both attacking tries fail cleanly. Pushing the pawn removes the king's cover and invites the rear checks. Interposing the rook on the third rank allows the exchange, and the resulting king-and-pawn ending is drawn.

A NUANCE Philidor himself got wrong: he believed the third-rank defence was the ONLY way to draw. Karstedt showed in 1897, and Berger elaborated, that this is not strictly true — other defences exist, though they are more complicated. The method is the best practical one, not the unique one.
